ear 98%", "Incumbent former governor in a D+6 state, with no public general-election polling"], "rationale": "Hickenlooper's overwhelming financial advantage, a unanimous Safe/Solid Democratic consensus from ten raters, and markets pricing his win near 98% all point to a comfortable incumbent victory in a state leaning Democratic by six points. With no public polls available, the forecast rests on those fundamentals, which show no sign of a competitive race.", "watch": "A late wave of Republican outside spending or a sharply nationalized environment could tighten the race, though no such spending has materialized.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent John Hickenlooper (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's Senate race pairs first-term incumbent John Hickenlooper, a former governor, against Republican challenger Mark Baisley in a state that has leaned Democratic by about six points in recent elections. No public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on fundamentals: Hickenlooper has raised roughly $8.1 million to Baisley's $138,000, holds a large cash advantage, and faces no outside spending against him. Every major race rater, from the Cook Political Report to Sabato's Crystal Ball, places the contest in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and prediction markets price a Hickenlooper win near 98 percent.\n\nThe candidates met in an October debate covering Trump, fuel prices and Social Security, but nothing suggests the race has tightened. Baisley's low profile and minimal fundraising leave little room to close the gap in the final weeks. A strong Republican national environment is the main remaining variable, though even that has not moved expert or market assessments.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9917, "margin": 17.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9895, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9916, "margin": 1